# Engineering

No application stack is present yet. Inspect the tree before assuming language, framework, or directory layout. When code lands, follow it; then update this rule with facts.

## Working rules

- Read existing code and tests before adding abstractions.
- Reuse established patterns; do not introduce a parallel architecture.
- Smallest coherent change. No drive-by refactors.
- Preserve backwards compatibility unless the task says otherwise.
- Error handling must match existing style once one exists.
- Do not modify old applied migrations unless project policy explicitly allows it.
- Prefer reversible schema changes. Separate destructive cleanup from compatibility migrations.
- Never execute destructive production database operations.

## When CRM domain code appears

Treat as HIGH until proven otherwise: authentication, authorization, tenant isolation, PII, exports, payments/billing, webhooks, financial calculations, file uploads.

If the app becomes multi-tenant, every query, job, cache key, export, WebSocket path, log line, and admin tool must stay tenant-scoped.

If money is calculated, do not use binary floating-point for currency; consider rounding, timezones, idempotency, and auditability.

## External integrations

Idempotency, retries, timeouts, partial failure, duplicate/out-of-order events, signatures, replay, rate limits, API-version compatibility. Never make irreversible production calls to "test."
